import{_ as h,o as s,c as n,ao as o,j as t,a}from"./chunks/framework.B7UvRKha.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"The project chat","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/chat.md","filePath":"guide/chat.md","lastUpdated":null}'),d={name:"guide/chat.md"},r={class:"MathJax",jax:"SVG",overflow:"linebreak"},i={style:{"vertical-align":"-0.025ex"},xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg",width:"5.715ex",height:"1.717ex",role:"img",focusable:"false",viewBox:"0 -748 2526 759"},l={class:"MathJax",jax:"SVG",overflow:"linebreak"},c={style:{"vertical-align":"-0.025ex"},xmlns:"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg",width:"1.9ex",height:"1.717ex",role:"img",focusable:"false",viewBox:"0 -748 840 759"};function w(g,e,m,p,u,f){return s(),n("div",null,[e[21]||(e[21]=o('<h1 id="The-project-chat" tabindex="-1">The project chat <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-project-chat" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The project chat {#The-project-chat}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The Chat drawer (<code>Ctrl+Shift+K</code>) shows the messages of one Slack channel: the channel the selected row&#39;s folder maps to. The records the window holds are listed oldest first, and the newest sits at the bottom. The view follows the newest, so a message that arrives takes the bottom line and lifts the rest. An empty channel shows <code>no messages</code>.</p><p>ArrowUp and ArrowDown (<code>chat.mark_older</code>, <code>chat.mark_newer</code>) mark one message, starting at the newest, and the view moves to show it. The mark is a lighter band across the message with a blue bar at its left edge; it changes nothing else about the message. A scroll that leaves the mark out of the drawer moves it to the nearest message still in view.</p><p>ArrowRight (<code>chat.thread_open</code>) opens the marked message&#39;s thread, a message with no replies included: the thread shows the message and its replies, oldest first, and Escape closes it, with the mark back on the message. Changing the row or hiding the drawer closes it too. A thread is read when it is opened, not watched: a reply made on the phone shows when the thread is opened again, and <code>N replies</code> on a message is not live either. Each open takes about 8 Slack reads for a long thread, more when Slack sends short pages, plus the lookups of the authors&#39; names. A thread shows up to 156 messages, the parent included: past that the newest replies are not shown, and the title says so. While a thread is open the channel&#39;s older messages give way to it, never its newest 100. A refused read is asked again after a few seconds (thirty when Slack rate limits), and the title says why. A message begun while a thread is open is a reply in that thread (the line under the draft reads <code>reply in thread of</code> the message&#39;s time), and one begun in the channel stays the channel&#39;s when a thread opens; a draft keeps where it was begun through closing the thread, changing the row and a reconnect. After your own reply is posted and the thread is open, it is read again, which costs about what opening it did (about 8 reads for a long thread), so it shows the parent alone for a moment.</p><p>To read older messages, scroll up: PageUp and PageDown move a third of the drawer, Alt+Up, Alt+Down and the mouse wheel one line. A reader scrolled up stays where they are: a message that arrives, is edited or is deleted does not move the view, and a message deleted at the reader&#39;s place stays there as <code>(message deleted)</code>; the next one takes the place only when a new snapshot or the trim drops it. <code>End</code> (<code>chat.latest</code>) returns to the newest and follows it again, and scrolling down to the newest does the same. One case moves the view: deleting or shortening the newest message can bring the newest wholly into view, which returns the reader to following. Scrolling to the oldest message reads older pages from Slack, up to the 256 messages the drawer keeps; the title says when a refused or empty read stops it, when Slack&#39;s plan hides older messages and when the full drawer leaves older ones unshown. Messages older than the newest 100 are as read: an edit or delete of one does not reach the drawer. The drawer drops the oldest past 256, never the message the reader is on or a newer one, so a reader on the oldest message stays there while the drawer holds up to 276; past 276 the oldest go anyway and the view moves to the next message that is kept. Switching to another row&#39;s channel starts at its newest. Switching back to one of the last two channels shown draws the messages the drawer held for it as soon as the hub answers for the row, with <code>reading…</code> in the title until it is read again. The read returns the channel&#39;s newest messages, which replace the newest held; the older ones kept stay as read, so an edit or delete of one, or a change to a thread&#39;s reply count, made while the channel was away does not reach the drawer for as long as the channel is one of the last two. The drawer keeps those messages in memory when the Chat drawer is hidden.</p><p>The drawer is dark, as Slack&#39;s desktop app is in dark mode: light gray text on a near-black ground. Each group of messages starts with a header line: the author&#39;s name in bold, a small gray badge for a post that came from a bot (<code>APP</code>), the drawer (<code>DRAWER</code>) or a row (<code>ROW</code>), and the time in small gray text, on a 12-hour clock (<code>2:14 PM</code>, whatever the day). The badge comes from where Slack says the post came from, never from the text, so a name or message that reads <code>[drawer]</code> or <code>ROW</code> shows no badge. The text follows as typed.</p><p>The first message of each local day, and the oldest message held, has a day divider above its header: the day&#39;s name (<code>Today</code>, <code>Yesterday</code>, or <code>Monday, November 9th</code>, with the year after it for another year) centered in a small outlined box with a thin line running out to each side. The divider is not text: a press or a drag never selects it, and a copy never includes it. The day is your local day, found with one fixed offset from UTC for every message, so near a daylight saving change <code>Today</code> or <code>Yesterday</code> can begin an hour from where your clock puts it.</p><p>A message that has replies has a line in Slack&#39;s style: <code>2 replies</code> (<code>1 reply</code>) in blue and, when the daemon knows it, the last reply in gray (<code>Last reply today at 2:44 PM</code>, <code>yesterday at …</code>, <code>Nov 9 at …</code>, with the year for another year).</p><p>A message that Slack marks as edited ends with <code>(edited)</code> in small gray type, after its last word (on its own line after a code block or a quote). A deleted message shows none.</p><p>Messages from one author in a row share one header, as in Slack: a message with the same author and origin as the one before it, on the same local day and less than five minutes after it, has no header of its own and its text follows the last one&#39;s. The first message of each group has a small colored square in the margin at the left, a color chosen from eight by the author (so two authors can share one); the text of every message starts right of that margin. Mark a message that has no header and its time (<code>2:01</code>, without AM or PM) shows in the margin beside it. The five minutes are Slack&#39;s rule as remembered, and have not been checked against the desktop app. A page of older messages that arrives above the top message can take that message&#39;s header and day divider off, which moves that message and every message below it up by their height. The reverse also happens: the oldest message held always shows a header and a divider, so when the oldest messages go (past 276), the one that becomes the oldest gains both, and the messages below it move down.</p><h2 id="Which-channel" tabindex="-1">Which channel <a class="header-anchor" href="#Which-channel" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which channel {#Which-channel}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The hub&#39;s <code>chat.toml</code> maps a folder to a channel; a row whose folder is mapped shows that channel. How the mapping is written is in <a href="/ship-of-tools/dev/ref/config#chat.toml-and-chat-secrets.toml">the chat configuration</a>. The drawer follows the selected row. Its title is <code>chat · &lt;channel id&gt;</code> (the id shows only while a channel is watched) followed by what the drawer is waiting for or why it shows nothing:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">the title says</th><th style="text-align:right;">meaning</th><th style="text-align:right;">asked again</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>waiting for this row&#39;s listing</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the window has not listed the selected row yet</td><td style="text-align:right;">every frame</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>not connected</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the row&#39;s computer is not connected</td><td style="text-align:right;">when it connects</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>reading…</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the hub is being asked, or the channel is being read</td><td style="text-align:right;"></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">(nothing after the channel)</td><td style="text-align:right;">the channel was read whole</td><td style="text-align:right;"></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>the row&#39;s folder maps to no channel</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the folder is not in <code>chat.toml</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">at a link change or another row</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>the hub has no usable chat configuration</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>chat.toml</code> is missing or invalid; the hub&#39;s daemon log says why, starting with the file&#39;s name</td><td style="text-align:right;">at a link change</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>the chat runs on the hub, not on &lt;host&gt;</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the row&#39;s computer is not the hub</td><td style="text-align:right;">at a link change</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>the row is gone or was replaced</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the row&#39;s id changed</td><td style="text-align:right;">when the listing gives the row a new id, or at a link change</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>the hub could not answer: &lt;reason&gt;</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the hub refused for another reason</td><td style="text-align:right;">after a minute</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>this daemon has no chat: update it</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the row&#39;s daemon is too old</td><td style="text-align:right;">at a link change</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>the hub&#39;s chat service is not running</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the hub&#39;s log says why</td><td style="text-align:right;">each minute</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>not up to date: &lt;reason&gt;</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the channel could not be read whole (<code>no snapshot</code> when the hub never answered)</td><td style="text-align:right;">each minute</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>the row is gone or was replaced</code>, <code>the row now maps to another channel</code> or <code>the row&#39;s folder maps to no channel</code>, after the channel</td><td style="text-align:right;">the hub refused the watch for good</td><td style="text-align:right;">at a row or link change</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Slack&#39;s-markup" tabindex="-1">Slack&#39;s markup <a class="header-anchor" href="#Slack&#39;s-markup" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Slack&#39;s markup {#Slack&#39;s-markup}&quot;">​</a></h2>',14)),t("p",null,[e[2]||(e[2]=a("Slack's inline styles show as styles. ",-1)),e[3]||(e[3]=t("code",null,"*bold*",-1)),e[4]||(e[4]=a(", ",-1)),e[5]||(e[5]=t("code",null,"_italic_",-1)),e[6]||(e[6]=a(" and ",-1)),e[7]||(e[7]=t("code",null,"~strike~",-1)),e[8]||(e[8]=a(" are drawn bold, slanted and struck through, and ",-1)),t("mjx-container",r,[(s(),n("svg",i,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="scale(1,-1)"><g data-mml-node="math" data-latex="`code`"><g data-mml-node="mo" data-latex="`"><path data-c="2035" d="M60 707C60 699 62 692 65 686L219 430L253 430L136 725C129 740 117 748 100 748C77 748 60 729 60 707Z"></path></g><g data-mml-node="mi" data-latex="c" transform="translate(311,0)"><path data-c="1D450" d="M328 325C328 300 341 287 368 287C404 287 427 318 427 354C427 410 368 442 308 442C239 442 176 412 122 353C68 294 41 229 41 159C41 61 106-11 204-11C257-11 304 2 345 27C379 48 404 69 420 90C427 99 430 106 430 109C430 120 425 126 414 126C409 126 404 122 398 114C365 70 324 42 277 30C246 22 223 18 206 18C149 18 121 53 121 122C121 184 150 274 174 317C198 361 249 413 308 413C346 413 372 402 386 381C355 378 328 358 328 325Z"></path></g><g data-mml-node="mi" data-latex="o" transform="translate(744,0)"><path data-c="1D45C" d="M308 442C239 442 176 412 122 353C68 294 41 229 41 159C41 63 106-11 202-11C272-11 334 19 388 78C442 137 469 201 469 272C469 369 405 442 308 442M389 310C389 287 384 255 374 213C353 130 319 73 272 42C248 26 225 18 203 18C149 18 121 65 121 122C121 180 155 288 178 324C216 383 259 413 307 413C361 413 389 367 389 310Z"></path></g><g data-mml-node="mi" data-latex="d" transform="translate(1229,0)"><path data-c="1D451" d="M429 632L370 389C349 426 320 445 281 445C216 445 159 412 109 345C63 283 40 218 40 151C40 63 91-11 175-11C218-11 260 12 300 59C311 21 345-11 392-11C461-11 483 71 498 145C498 154 493 159 483 159C474 159 468 152 465 138C445 59 421 19 394 19C377 19 368 33 368 60C368 75 370 91 374 107L516 679L516 683C513 690 507 694 499 694C476 694 434 690 374 683C363 682 357 674 357 660C357 650 366 645 384 645C403 645 429 646 429 632M341 376C351 355 356 341 356 332C355 328 354 323 353 316L304 122C301 111 294 99 285 87C248 42 212 19 177 19C138 19 118 49 118 108C118 132 124 168 136 216C157 301 187 359 224 390C244 407 263 415 282 415C309 415 329 402 341 376Z"></path></g><g data-mml-node="mi" data-latex="e" transform="translate(1749,0)"><path data-c="1D452" d="M124 129C124 153 129 186 139 227L188 227C253 227 303 235 339 250C372 264 394 284 405 309C412 326 415 342 415 355C415 410 363 442 307 442C268 442 229 432 190 412C113 372 46 281 46 171C46 69 105-11 204-11C257-11 304 2 345 27C379 48 404 69 420 90C427 99 430 106 430 109C430 120 425 126 414 126C409 126 404 122 398 114C365 70 324 42 277 30C246 22 223 18 206 18C149 18 124 72 124 129M375 355C375 289 311 256 182 256L147 256C166 322 194 366 232 387C262 404 287 413 307 413C343 413 375 391 375 355Z"></path></g><g data-mml-node="mo" data-latex="`" transform="translate(2215,0)"><path data-c="2035" d="M60 707C60 699 62 692 65 686L219 430L253 430L136 725C129 740 117 748 100 748C77 748 60 729 60 707Z"></path></g></g></g>',1)])]))]),e[9]||(e[9]=a(" is drawn in a monospace face, in orange, on a bordered box; the marks themselves are not shown, and a selection or a copy of the message gives the text as shown. The rules for where a style starts and stops are the drawer's reading of Slack's and have not yet been compared with the desktop app side by side: a style opens at the start of the text or after a space or punctuation mark and before something that is not a space, and closes at the next matching mark on the same line before a space, punctuation or the end. A mark in the middle of a word (",-1)),e[10]||(e[10]=t("code",null,"2*3*4",-1)),e[11]||(e[11]=a(", ",-1)),e[12]||(e[12]=t("code",null,"snake_case_name",-1)),e[13]||(e[13]=a("), an unclosed one, and one closed on a later line stay as typed, and so does a run of backticks where no code span is open (two that end one span and begin the next disappear: ",-1)),t("mjx-container",l,[(s(),n("svg",c,[...e[1]||(e[1]=[o('<g stroke="currentColor" fill="currentColor" stroke-width="0" transform="scale(1,-1)"><g data-mml-node="math" data-latex="`a"><g data-mml-node="mo" data-latex="`"><path data-c="2035" d="M60 707C60 699 62 692 65 686L219 430L253 430L136 725C129 740 117 748 100 748C77 748 60 729 60 707Z"></path></g><g data-mml-node="mi" data-latex="a" transform="translate(311,0)"><path data-c="1D44E" d="M498 144C498 153 493 158 482 158C474 158 468 151 465 137C445 58 421 18 394 18C377 18 368 32 368 60C368 73 372 97 381 132L438 357C443 376 445 387 445 392C445 412 434 422 412 422C391 422 377 410 370 387C349 424 319 442 281 442C216 442 159 409 109 343C63 281 40 217 40 150C40 63 91-11 175-11C218-11 260 12 300 58C311 20 345-11 392-11C461-11 482 70 498 144M341 374C350 353 355 339 355 330C355 326 354 321 353 314L304 122C301 111 294 99 285 87C248 41 212 18 177 18C138 18 118 48 118 107C118 131 124 167 136 215C157 300 187 357 224 388C244 405 263 413 282 413C309 413 329 400 341 374Z"></path></g></g></g>',1)])]))]),e[14]||(e[14]=a("b```shows",-1)),e[15]||(e[15]=t("code",null,"ab",-1)),e[16]||(e[16]=a("in one box). A mark followed by the same mark does not start a style, so",-1)),e[17]||(e[17]=t("code",null,"**x**",-1)),e[18]||(e[18]=a("shows",-1)),e[19]||(e[19]=t("code",null,"x",-1)),e[20]||(e[20]=a(" in bold between two asterisks. Code shows no other style inside it, and a style around code is dropped.",-1))]),e[22]||(e[22]=o('<p>A fenced code block (three backticks, then three again) shows its lines in a monospace face on one bordered panel that runs from just left of the text to the drawer&#39;s right edge; the fences are not shown, and nothing inside is read as a style or a mention. A fence that is never closed stays as typed. A line that starts with <code>&gt;</code> is a quote and shows without it, beside a gray bar left of the text, one bar for consecutive quote lines; <code>&gt;&gt;&gt;</code> quotes its line and all that follow, and styles and mentions work inside a quote. An empty line inside a block or a quote keeps its place in the panel or the bar. Neither indents its text, so a line that wraps starts at the text&#39;s left edge, and a list shows as Slack sends it (<code>•</code>, <code>1.</code>) with no hanging indent. These rules, too, are the drawer&#39;s reading of Slack&#39;s and have not yet been compared with the desktop app side by side.</p><p>Links and mentions show as Slack shows them, in blue. A link shows its label, or its address when it has none; the address is never shown beside a label, and a link is not clickable. A mention shows <code>@</code> and the person&#39;s name on a tinted slug, else <code>@</code> and the label when the message carries one, else <code>@</code> and the person&#39;s id. The daemon looks the names up when the drawer reads a page of the channel (within ten seconds, so a name it could not reach in time shows as the label or the id); a message that arrives live is named only from what the daemon already looked up, never by a request of its own, and so is its author&#39;s name; a person not yet read shows as the label or the id (an author as no name) until the page is read again. A name is looked up once while the daemon runs and is not refreshed. A token inside code stays as typed. A channel shows <code>#</code> and its name, else its label, else its id. The daemon looks a channel up the same way, but only a channel mention that carries no label, and it counts toward the same sixteen names a message can have, people before channels. A private channel shows its label or its id, never its name, as Slack shows a channel you may not be in; whether Slack answers for a channel is not yet confirmed, and when it does not the channel shows as before; <code>@here</code>, <code>@channel</code> and <code>@everyone</code> show by name, and a group shows its label. A date shows the plain text Slack sends with it. A label is one line, whatever it holds. Any other markup the drawer does not read is shown as the markup text in blue. Text that looks like a header or a badge inside a message stays body text, however it is styled.</p><h2 id="Deleted-messages" tabindex="-1">Deleted messages <a class="header-anchor" href="#Deleted-messages" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Deleted messages {#Deleted-messages}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A message deleted while the drawer watches stays in its place, with its author, time and origin, and reads <code>(message deleted)</code>. It counts toward the 256 messages the drawer keeps. A new snapshot (the window reads one after any loss of the chat feed) drops it, since the snapshot holds only what Slack still has, except a deleted message older than the snapshot&#39;s oldest when the snapshot reaches back to what the drawer holds, which stays; a snapshot that does not drops it too.</p><h2 id="When-Slack-is-unreachable" tabindex="-1">When Slack is unreachable <a class="header-anchor" href="#When-Slack-is-unreachable" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When Slack is unreachable {#When-Slack-is-unreachable}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The title does not show the state of the Slack connection. A socket outage shows only when it costs a snapshot, as <code>not up to date</code>. The drawer keeps the records it holds until a new snapshot replaces them. <code>no messages</code> appears only for a channel that was read whole and holds none.</p><h2 id="When-the-window&#39;s-link-drops" tabindex="-1">When the window&#39;s link drops <a class="header-anchor" href="#When-the-window&#39;s-link-drops" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When the window&#39;s link drops {#When-the-window&#39;s-link-drops}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The records the drawer holds stay on screen and the title says <code>not connected</code>. When the link to the computer comes back, the window watches the channel again and reads a new snapshot, which replaces the records; a lost chat feed on a link that stays up does the same. A draft is kept through both.</p><h2 id="The-draft" tabindex="-1">The draft <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-draft" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The draft {#The-draft}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>With the drawer focused, typing writes a draft at its foot: <code>&gt;</code> and the last three rows, then where it goes (<code>to &lt;channel&gt;</code>, or <code>reply in thread of</code> the parent&#39;s time for a draft begun in a thread, and for the next one while a thread is open) and, if the draft is longer, how many rows are not shown (a line wider than the drawer wraps, and each wrapped row counts). <code>Shift+Enter</code> (<code>chat.newline</code>) starts another line, and a paste is added whole. There is one draft per row, and where it goes (the channel, or the thread open then) is fixed when its first character is typed, even if you move the view afterwards. A draft stops at what a post can carry (8 KiB, counting <code>&amp;</code>, <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> as five, four and four bytes); a key or paste past it is refused whole and the status line says so. Control characters other than tab are dropped. A draft is lost when its row goes or the window closes.</p><h2 id="Posting" tabindex="-1">Posting <a class="header-anchor" href="#Posting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Posting {#Posting}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>Enter</code> (<code>chat.send</code>) posts the draft once, to the channel or thread its compose line names. The post carries the name in <code>[chat] name</code> of <code>settings.toml</code> (see <a href="./../ref/config">the settings reference</a>); with no name set, nothing is sent: the foot of the drawer becomes the prompt <code>Name for your chat posts:</code> and the keys type the name instead of the draft. <code>Enter</code> saves it to <code>settings.toml</code> (the file the window loaded, or the per-user one, made if missing) and uses it at once, for every row and project in the window, and the draft stays: the next <code>Enter</code> posts it. <code>Escape</code> cancels and keeps the draft. A name is refused, with the reason on the status line and the prompt open, when it is empty, holds a quote or a backslash, or is longer than 160 bytes (the prompt takes no more at 160 bytes), and a paste that holds a newline or another control character is refused whole, never joined. The prompt ends, with the draft kept, when you change the row, hide the drawer, the link to the hub changes, or the row has no mapped channel; scrolling, the mark and a thread&#39;s opening still work while it is open. A settings file the window will not edit safely (one that is not valid TOML, or spells <code>[chat]</code> or <code>name</code> in some other way) is left as it is, and the status line says how to add the name by hand. The name is asked once per settings file; to change it later, edit <code>[chat] name</code> and restart the window. While the post waits the draft is frozen: a key or <code>Enter</code> is refused with <code>posting…</code> on the status line. The draft is cleared only by a reply that proves the post was made. Any other outcome keeps it, and the status line says why: <code>not posted</code> with the reason (a <code>bad request</code> names <code>[chat] name</code>), a wait when Slack is rate limiting (<code>Enter</code> is refused until it passes), or <code>may have been posted</code> when the answer was lost, in which case look at the channel before sending it again: a post is never sent a second time on its own. A typed <code>&lt;</code> posts as itself, and a post cannot mention or ping anyone: <code>@here</code> and <code>&lt;!channel&gt;</code> arrive as plain text. A window posts only to a hub that is updated to escape text; to an older hub it says <code>the hub must be updated to post</code> and keeps the draft.</p><h2 id="Selecting" tabindex="-1">Selecting <a class="header-anchor" href="#Selecting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Selecting {#Selecting}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Press the left mouse button on a message&#39;s text and drag to select within that message: the selection stays in the message where you began, and the name and time line is not selectable. A click on the name and time line, in the margin, or anywhere else, selects nothing, and a click in the messages still focuses the drawer. Any other click clears the selection, and so does a change to the message (an edit or a delete), a new window width, opening a thread, and changing the row; moving the mark does not. A chat selection is never shared with the agent; only the preview&#39;s text and your drawing are.</p><h2 id="Copying" tabindex="-1">Copying <a class="header-anchor" href="#Copying" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Copying {#Copying}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>Ctrl+C</code> or <code>Ctrl+Shift+C</code> (<code>Cmd+C</code> on macOS; <code>chat.copy_selection</code>) with the Chat drawer focused copies the selected text exactly as shown: a link&#39;s label, a mention&#39;s name and a style&#39;s text without its marks, a code block&#39;s lines without its fences and a quote&#39;s without its <code>&gt;</code> (an empty line in either copies as an empty line, with no added space), markup the drawer does not read as the markup text, <code>&amp;lt;</code> and <code>&amp;gt;</code> as <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code>, and a deleted message as <code>(message deleted)</code>, with the message&#39;s own line breaks and none from the wrap. With no selection it copies the marked message&#39;s text, not its name and time line (a message without a header copies its whole text). <code>(edited)</code> is Slack&#39;s mark and not the message&#39;s text, so no copy holds it. With neither, the status line says there is nothing to copy. Ctrl+C in the agent pane, the Terminal and the Julia REPL is unchanged.</p><h2 id="Mail-to-the-row" tabindex="-1">Mail to the row <a class="header-anchor" href="#Mail-to-the-row" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Mail to the row {#Mail-to-the-row}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When the hub&#39;s <code>chat.toml</code> sets <code>mail_after</code>, the daemon files each new post a channel member makes (or the drawer makes for you) in a mapped channel as one marked line of mail to the row mapped to its folder, with no window open. A free Claude row is woken, a busy one reads it at the end of its turn, and a Codex row gets it unwoken. Today this covers the posts the daemon sees live and files at once; a recovery pass finds the rest. A post made while the daemon was down or the Slack connection was lost, and a post whose filing was refused (the row had no live session for ten minutes, say), are found by the first pass after the connection&#39;s next hello, or after the refusal, or after 6 hours at the latest (while the row stays absent, a pass is tried again after 30 seconds, doubling to 10 minutes), and the daemon log shows each filing and each pass as one line. A pass reads the channel back from now to the channel&#39;s floor (about one read a second and a half, and one more for each thread with a reply since), files at most 200 posts and goes again at once for more. Slack hides older history on some plans, and a pass reads what Slack gives. Slack hands each event to one connection, so any other program that opens a connection with the chat app&#39;s token while the hub delivers (a probe, or a daemon from before the single listener) takes some events from it with no sign that any was lost, and those posts wait for the next hello (a few hours) or the 6-hour sweep, whichever comes first, not for the next pass of any kind. A pass and the drawer share Slack&#39;s limit on reads: a pass at about 40 reads a minute leaves the drawer the rest of Slack&#39;s history allowance, and when Slack tells a pass to wait, the drawer&#39;s reads wait for the same time (a post is not held by it). A restart files nothing twice, because the daemon reads the inboxes at its start and files nothing until it has.</p><h3 id="The-row&#39;s-reply" tabindex="-1">The row&#39;s reply <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-row&#39;s-reply" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The row&#39;s reply {#The-row&#39;s-reply}&quot;">​</a></h3><p>A row&#39;s agent replies with <code>sot-fe chat post [--thread &lt;ts&gt;] (&lt;text&gt; | --stdin)</code>, run in the row. It posts for the row it runs in, under the handle the row&#39;s session joined with, shown as <code>&lt;handle&gt; (row via Ship of Tools)</code>, into the channel the row is mapped to: into the thread named by <code>--thread</code> (the <code>thread=</code> value of the mail&#39;s second line), else as a new message. The skills tell an agent to give the reply on standard input. It asks the daemon for the row&#39;s channel and sends one post, never a second: a post whose answer is lost is reported as <code>may exist</code>, and the agent is told never to send it again. It prints <code>posted &lt;ts&gt;</code> and exits 0; exits 2 with <code>refused &lt;code&gt;</code> or <code>limited, wait &lt;n&gt; s</code> when nothing was posted; and exits 3 with <code>unknown</code> or <code>no answer</code> when the post may exist, in which case the agent never posts again and reads the thread first. A reply is public to every member of the channel, so the agent is told to put nothing in it that the lab&#39;s rules on public text and on partners&#39; work keep out of shared text. The daemon refuses a person&#39;s post from a connection that is not a window, and a row&#39;s post under any name but the row&#39;s own handle; the declared role is the connection&#39;s own word, so this keeps one account&#39;s programs apart and is not a security boundary. A reply is not mail: it does not come back to the row.</p><h2 id="Moving-the-hub" tabindex="-1">Moving the hub <a class="header-anchor" href="#Moving-the-hub" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Moving the hub {#Moving-the-hub}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The chat listens on one computer for the whole comm folder: the daemon that holds the lock in <code>chat/</code> of the comm folder (<code>~/.sot-comm/chat/</code> by default). To move the hub, edit <code>hosts.toml</code> and start the new hub&#39;s daemon; nothing else. The old hub&#39;s chat notices the new topology within a minute, waits for the one message it is filing to be filed or refused (refused when the topology now names another hub; the new hub&#39;s first pass finds it), and stops listening, and a new hub whose start finds the lock still held tries again each minute and listens once it is free. A computer that was lost should free its lock when the file server&#39;s lease runs out, in about two minutes; that rests on the lease and has not been measured. A computer that becomes the hub starts its chat when its daemon next starts. Posts made in the minute around a move can reach the row hours late: until the old hub steps down its connection takes some of the app&#39;s events, refuses them, and the new hub&#39;s connection never sees them, so the new hub finds those posts at its next hello or at the 6-hour sweep.</p>',22))])}const k=h(d,[["render",w]]);export{b as __pageData,k as default};
